# How Learning Data Structures and Algorithms Changed the Way I Solve Problems

When I first started learning Data Structures and Algorithms (DSA), I thought it was mainly about writing code, remembering different algorithms and solving difficult programming questions. The terms like arrays, linked lists, stacks, queues and searching algorithms looked simple in theory, but actually understanding where and why to use them was a different story. After going through the first few weeks of the **Data Structures & Algorithms using C++, C and Python - 2026** course on Infosys Springboard, I started looking at DSA in a slightly different way.

I realised that DSA is not just a programming topic. It is actually a way of thinking and solving problems in a more organised manner.

## Understanding the Problem Before Writing Code

One of the first things I noticed while learning DSA is that writing code is not always the first step. Before coding, we need to understand the problem properly.

For example, suppose I have a list of 1,000 names and I need to find whether a particular name exists in it. The easiest approach that comes to mind is checking every name one by one. This works, but it can take a lot of time when the list becomes very large.

This is where algorithmic thinking becomes important. Instead of immediately writing code, we start asking questions like: Can I make this faster? Is the data already sorted? Can I use a better searching technique?

Learning about algorithms such as linear search and binary search helped me understand this difference. Both can solve the same basic problem, but the way they solve it and the time they require can be very different.

## Data Structures Are About Organising Information

Another important thing I learned is that data structures are basically different ways of organising data depending on what we want to do with it.

Arrays are probably one of the first data structures most students learn. They are simple and useful when we need to store elements in a fixed order. But when we need frequent insertion and deletion, other structures can sometimes be more suitable.

For example, a **stack** follows the Last In, First Out (LIFO) principle. At first, this sounds like just another definition that we have to remember for an exam. But when I thought about real-life examples, it became much easier to understand. A stack of plates is a simple example. The last plate placed on top is usually the first one we remove.

Similarly, a **queue** follows First In, First Out (FIFO). A queue of people waiting for their turn is an example we see almost every day. The person who comes first generally gets served first.

These examples made DSA feel less like memorising definitions and more like understanding systems that already exist around us.

## Python Made Experimenting Easier

One thing I personally liked about the course was getting exposure to DSA using different programming languages, especially Python.

Python has a relatively simple syntax, so I can focus more on understanding the logic instead of worrying too much about complicated syntax. For a beginner, this makes experimenting with algorithms much easier.

For example, creating a list in Python is very straightforward. I can quickly add, remove or access elements and test how different operations work. This does not mean that C or C++ are less useful. In fact, learning the same concept in different languages helped me understand that the underlying logic of a data structure remains mostly the same, even though the implementation can look different.

This was an interesting lesson for me: programming languages are tools, but the actual problem-solving logic is what matters more.

## The Importance of Time and Space Complexity

Before studying DSA, I honestly did not pay much attention to how efficient my code was as long as it produced the correct output. But DSA introduced me to the idea that two programs can give the same answer while having completely different performance.

This is where time complexity and space complexity become important.

An algorithm that works perfectly for 10 elements might become extremely slow when the input contains a million elements. Understanding concepts such as Big-O notation gives us a basic idea of how an algorithm will behave as the amount of data increases.

I found this concept slightly confusing in the beginning, especially when comparing different complexities. But after looking at examples and trying to analyse simple programs myself, it started making more sense.

## DSA Is Useful Beyond Programming

The biggest takeaway for me is that DSA is not limited to coding interviews or competitive programming. The way of thinking behind DSA can be applied to many situations.

Suppose I have a large number of tasks to complete. I have to decide which task should be done first, which tasks are more important and how I can organise everything efficiently. In a way, this is similar to choosing an appropriate data structure or algorithm.

Even something as simple as finding a particular file on a computer involves searching and organisation. Online shopping platforms, navigation applications, social media and search engines all deal with huge amounts of data and need efficient algorithms to work properly.

We may not notice these algorithms while using these applications, but they are working behind the scenes.

## My Learning Experience

The first few weeks of the Infosys Springboard course gave me a better foundation for understanding DSA. I would not say that I have mastered everything already. There are still concepts that take time to understand, and sometimes even a small programming problem can take longer than expected.

However, I have started becoming more comfortable with breaking a problem into smaller parts before trying to solve it.

Earlier, if I saw a programming problem, my first thought was usually, "How do I code this?" Now I try to think, "What exactly is the problem, what data do I have, and what would be the most suitable way to organise and process it?"

That small change in thinking is probably the most valuable thing I have gained from learning DSA so far.

## Conclusion

Data Structures and Algorithms can initially feel like a collection of complicated concepts and definitions. But once we start connecting them with real-world situations, they become much easier to understand.

My experience with the Infosys Springboard course has shown me that DSA is not simply about learning arrays, stacks, queues or searching techniques. It is about learning how to approach a problem logically and find a solution that is not only correct but also efficient.

I still have a lot more to learn, especially as the topics become more advanced. But after these first few weeks, I feel that I have started building the right foundation. And for me, that is what self-learning is really about — not knowing everything immediately, but becoming better at understanding something every time I practice it.
